<!--
PROVENANCE OF THE ANSWER KEY

There is no answer key in the course materials. This one was reconstructed from
the Handout 2 PowerPoint, and the confidence behind each item is not uniform.
Three tiers:

  CONFIRMED BY THE DECK (scenarios 1, 2).
    Slides 33-45 work these two through with the answer boxed, and the speaker
    notes state them outright: "something desirable is being added - ...
    Positive Reinforcement" (slide 38) and "Avoid sunburn = take away pain ...
    it would be Negative Reinforcement" (slide 45).

  DETERMINATE UNDER THE COURSE'S OWN RULES (scenarios 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12,
  13, 14, 15, 17, 18, 19, 20, and 16).
    These follow from the three-question method plus two rules the speaker
    notes state explicitly: "the behavior cannot also be the
    reinforcer/punisher" (slides 34, 41), and "getting a sunburn is not an
    action, it is the result of inaction" (slide 41). Question 3 asks what
    happens AFTER the behavior (slide 30). Two get independent corroboration:
    the notes name spanking as the canonical PP example (slide 25) and
    grounding-from-the-car as the canonical NP example (slide 27), which are
    scenarios 5 and 11.

  WORTH A SECOND OPINION (scenarios 3, 6, 10, 16).
    Flagged individually at each question below. Scenario 6 is a genuine
    coin-flip; 3, 10 and 16 are a single issue seen three times.

Distribution is PR 4 / NR 6 / PP 6 / NP 4, matching the handout's note that
"there are not equal numbers of each type". Note that the distribution does NOT
disambiguate scenario 6: keying it PP would give PR 4 / NR 6 / PP 7 / NP 3,
which is equally unequal.

These questions are auto-graded here, whereas on paper they were discussed in
Small Group and never recorded in the scoring workbook. A wrong key now costs a
student marks silently, so the flagged items deserve a decision rather than a
default.
-->

<Vertical id="bmod_h2_scenario_bank" title="Handout 2 scenario bank">

  <CapaProblem id="bmod_h2_scenario_01" title="1. Congratulations" showanswer="never">
    <KeyGrader id="bmod_h2_scenario_01_grader">
      <p>Johnny scores a goal, and his friends congratulate him.</p>
      <ChoiceInput id="bmod_h2_scenario_01_input">
        <Key id="bmod_h2_scenario_01_pr">Positive Reinforcement</Key>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_01_nr">Negative Reinforcement</Distractor>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_01_pp">Positive Punishment</Distractor>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_01_np">Negative Punishment</Distractor>
      </ChoiceInput>
      <Explanation showWhen="answered"><Markdown>Behavior: *scoring a goal*. Johnny will try to score more often (increase), and his friends' congratulations are **added** afterwards.</Markdown></Explanation>
    </KeyGrader>
    <DemandHints id="bmod_h2_scenario_01_hints">
      <Hint>Question 1: what is the behavior? It has to be an action the person takes, not something that happens to them.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 2: will they do it MORE often or LESS often in future? More often is reinforcement; less often is punishment.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 3: after the behavior, is something ADDED or TAKEN AWAY? Added is positive; taken away is negative — including something unpleasant that is avoided or escaped.</Hint>
    </DemandHints>
  </CapaProblem>

  <CapaProblem id="bmod_h2_scenario_02" title="2. Sunscreen" showanswer="never">
    <KeyGrader id="bmod_h2_scenario_02_grader">
      <p>I put on sunscreen to avoid a sunburn.</p>
      <ChoiceInput id="bmod_h2_scenario_02_input">
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_02_pr">Positive Reinforcement</Distractor>
        <Key id="bmod_h2_scenario_02_nr">Negative Reinforcement</Key>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_02_pp">Positive Punishment</Distractor>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_02_np">Negative Punishment</Distractor>
      </ChoiceInput>
      <Explanation showWhen="answered"><Markdown>Behavior: *putting on sunscreen* — note that getting a sunburn is not an action, it is the result of inaction. You will do it more often (increase), and the pain of a sunburn is **taken away** by being avoided.</Markdown></Explanation>
    </KeyGrader>
    <DemandHints id="bmod_h2_scenario_02_hints">
      <Hint>Question 1: what is the behavior? It has to be an action the person takes, not something that happens to them.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 2: will they do it MORE often or LESS often in future? More often is reinforcement; less often is punishment.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 3: after the behavior, is something ADDED or TAKEN AWAY? Added is positive; taken away is negative — including something unpleasant that is avoided or escaped.</Hint>
    </DemandHints>
  </CapaProblem>

  <!-- REVIEW (natural consequence). Keyed PP: sticking your hand in a flame
       decreases, and a painful burn is added. Right within the course's
       framing, but strictly this is a NATURAL consequence - nobody arranges
       it - and behavior analysis distinguishes that from arranged punishment.
       Same issue as scenarios 10 and 16. The handout plainly treats all three
       as punishment, so that is what is keyed here. -->
  <CapaProblem id="bmod_h2_scenario_03" title="3. Flame" showanswer="never">
    <KeyGrader id="bmod_h2_scenario_03_grader">
      <p>You stick your hand in a flame and you get a painful burn.</p>
      <ChoiceInput id="bmod_h2_scenario_03_input">
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_03_pr">Positive Reinforcement</Distractor>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_03_nr">Negative Reinforcement</Distractor>
        <Key id="bmod_h2_scenario_03_pp">Positive Punishment</Key>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_03_np">Negative Punishment</Distractor>
      </ChoiceInput>
      <Explanation showWhen="answered"><Markdown>Behavior: *sticking your hand in a flame*. You will do it less often (decrease), and a painful burn is **added** afterwards.</Markdown></Explanation>
    </KeyGrader>
    <DemandHints id="bmod_h2_scenario_03_hints">
      <Hint>Question 1: what is the behavior? It has to be an action the person takes, not something that happens to them.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 2: will they do it MORE often or LESS often in future? More often is reinforcement; less often is punishment.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 3: after the behavior, is something ADDED or TAKEN AWAY? Added is positive; taken away is negative — including something unpleasant that is avoided or escaped.</Hint>
    </DemandHints>
  </CapaProblem>

  <CapaProblem id="bmod_h2_scenario_04" title="4. Late Paper" showanswer="never">
    <KeyGrader id="bmod_h2_scenario_04_grader">
      <p>Willow turns in their paper two days late, so they automatically get 20 points off.</p>
      <ChoiceInput id="bmod_h2_scenario_04_input">
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_04_pr">Positive Reinforcement</Distractor>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_04_nr">Negative Reinforcement</Distractor>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_04_pp">Positive Punishment</Distractor>
        <Key id="bmod_h2_scenario_04_np">Negative Punishment</Key>
      </ChoiceInput>
      <Explanation showWhen="answered"><Markdown>Behavior: *turning the paper in late*. Willow will do it less often (decrease), and 20 points are **taken away**.</Markdown></Explanation>
    </KeyGrader>
    <DemandHints id="bmod_h2_scenario_04_hints">
      <Hint>Question 1: what is the behavior? It has to be an action the person takes, not something that happens to them.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 2: will they do it MORE often or LESS often in future? More often is reinforcement; less often is punishment.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 3: after the behavior, is something ADDED or TAKEN AWAY? Added is positive; taken away is negative — including something unpleasant that is avoided or escaped.</Hint>
    </DemandHints>
  </CapaProblem>

  <CapaProblem id="bmod_h2_scenario_05" title="5. Spanking" showanswer="never">
    <KeyGrader id="bmod_h2_scenario_05_grader">
      <p>A child misbehaves and gets a spanking.</p>
      <ChoiceInput id="bmod_h2_scenario_05_input">
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_05_pr">Positive Reinforcement</Distractor>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_05_nr">Negative Reinforcement</Distractor>
        <Key id="bmod_h2_scenario_05_pp">Positive Punishment</Key>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_05_np">Negative Punishment</Distractor>
      </ChoiceInput>
      <Explanation showWhen="answered"><Markdown>Behavior: *misbehaving*. The child should misbehave less often (decrease), and a spanking is **added** afterwards.</Markdown></Explanation>
    </KeyGrader>
    <DemandHints id="bmod_h2_scenario_05_hints">
      <Hint>Question 1: what is the behavior? It has to be an action the person takes, not something that happens to them.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 2: will they do it MORE often or LESS often in future? More often is reinforcement; less often is punishment.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 3: after the behavior, is something ADDED or TAKEN AWAY? Added is positive; taken away is negative — including something unpleasant that is avoided or escaped.</Hint>
    </DemandHints>
  </CapaProblem>

  <!-- REVIEW (genuine coin-flip). Keyed NP on the reading that a demotion
       REMOVES rank and pay, which makes it parallel to Willow's lost points
       (4), Julie's lost car (11) and Brandon's lost lab membership (15) - all
       "you lose something you had".

       The case for PP: a demotion can equally be read as ADDING an aversive
       condition (lower status, worse duties).

       Nothing in the deck settles it and the distribution does not either.
       This is the single item most likely to be keyed wrong. -->
  <CapaProblem id="bmod_h2_scenario_06" title="6. Demotion" showanswer="never">
    <KeyGrader id="bmod_h2_scenario_06_grader">
      <p>You come to work late regularly, and you get demoted.</p>
      <ChoiceInput id="bmod_h2_scenario_06_input">
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_06_pr">Positive Reinforcement</Distractor>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_06_nr">Negative Reinforcement</Distractor>
        <Distractor id="bmod_h2_scenario_06_pp">Positive Punishment</Distractor>
        <Key id="bmod_h2_scenario_06_np">Negative Punishment</Key>
      </ChoiceInput>
      <Explanation showWhen="answered"><Markdown>Behavior: *coming to work late*. You will do it less often (decrease), and your rank and pay are **taken away**. Compare this with Willow's lost points and Julie's lost car — all three remove something you had.</Markdown></Explanation>
    </KeyGrader>
    <DemandHints id="bmod_h2_scenario_06_hints">
      <Hint>Question 1: what is the behavior? It has to be an action the person takes, not something that happens to them.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 2: will they do it MORE often or LESS often in future? More often is reinforcement; less often is punishment.</Hint>
      <Hint>Question 3: after the behavior, is something ADDED or TAKEN AWAY? Added is positive; taken away is negative — including something unpleasant that is avoided or escaped.</Hint>
    </DemandHints>
  </CapaProblem>

